[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 821,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 764,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126285,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 764,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adenocarcinoma of mammary gland type comprises a group of primary vulval tumours showing histopathological features identical to breast carcinomas.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:50:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 822,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 765,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126286,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8500/3 Adenocarcinoma of mammary gland type</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:50:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126287,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2C70.Z &amp; XH9FX2 Malignant neoplasms of vulva, unspecified &amp; Adenocarcinoma of mammary gland type</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:50:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126288,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> adenocarcinoma of anogenital mammary-like glands.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:50:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126289,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:50:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126290,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The most common location is the labia majora {28418164} [[WHO Classification of Tumours Editorial Board. Female genital tumours. Lyon (France): International Agency for Research on Cancer; 2020. (WHO classification of tumours series, 5th ed.; vol. 4). https://publications.iarc.who.int/592.]].</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:50:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126291,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most patients are postmenopausal and present with a vulval mass.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:50:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126292,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These are rare vulval neoplasms.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:50:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126293,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:50:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126294,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adenocarcinoma of mammary gland type is proposed to arise from anogenital mammary-like glands located in the anal and vulval area {28418164; 27759697}. Mutations in the genes of the PI3K/AKT cascade have been found in a few studied cases {28291131}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:50:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126295,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The macroscopic appearance is that of a solitary nodule with a fibrous cut surface, similar to breast carcinomas.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:50:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126296,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "<p>The most common type is adenocarcinoma, which is identical to mammary invasive ductal carcinoma and may (uncommonly) have an identifiable in&nbsp;situ component in the adjacent glands. Rare types include lobular, tubulolobular, mixed ductal and lobular, and mammary analogue secretory carcinoma. There is commonly expression of ER, PR, GATA3, and ERBB2 {27655126;16625074; 20505503; 16931966; 29672325}. Based on the immunoprofile, they can be classified like their breast counterparts into luminal&nbsp;A, luminal&nbsp;B, HER2-enriched, and basal-like subtypes {28418164}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-28T13:07:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 775,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126297,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 775,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>No data available</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:50:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 833,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 776,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126298,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 776,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Diagnostic molecular pathology is not clinically relevant in most cases. Secretory carcinoma harbours the characteristic <em>ETV6</em>::<em>NTRK3</em> fusion.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:50:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 836,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 779,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126299,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 779,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a solitary nodule with a fibrous cut surface, similar to breast carcinoma; histology consistent with that of invasive breast carcinoma.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> location usually the labia majora; association with lobular structures consistent with mammary differentiation; immunohistochemical expression of ER, PR, and ERBB2.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:50:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 834,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 777,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126300,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 777,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Staging follows the eighth edition of the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM classification.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:50:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 835,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 778,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 126301,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 159,
        "headingId": 778,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adenocarcinomas of mammary gland type are locally aggressive tumours that often metastasize to regional lymph nodes. Distant metastases are uncommon. Hormonal and anti-HER2 therapy may be helpful {21132401; 23030494}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T18:50:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]